Cynthia Michele Vanden Berg, Anchorage, AK.
I submit these comments regarding DeepGreen Cook Inlet SPV, LLC’s proposed DeepGreen Cook Inlet Subsea-Compute & Infrastructure Hub.

My primary concern is the potential introduction of new chemical, metal, electronic, polymer, and corrosion-related contaminants into Cook Inlet from hundreds of submerged industrial components and dozens of subsea computing pods.

The proposal involves approximately 330–350 marine hydrokinetic turbines, 66 subsea computing/data-center pods, subsea electrical and fiber infrastructure, and associated equipment. Before this project advances, FERC should require a comprehensive environmental review of the materials that would be placed underwater, how those materials degrade in seawater, what may be released during normal operation, and what could enter Cook Inlet during equipment failure.

1. Require a full chemical and materials inventory

DeepGreen should disclose the identity and quantity of environmentally relevant materials contained in turbines, data-center pods, cables, electrical systems, coatings, corrosion-control systems, and associated equipment.

The inventory should include, where present:

• coolants and heat-transfer fluids;
• dielectric and insulating fluids;
• hydraulic fluids and lubricants;
• batteries and electrolytes;
• fire-suppression chemicals;
• PFAS or other fluorinated materials;
• plastics and polymers;
• flame retardants;
• paints, epoxies, sealants, and antifouling coatings;
• copper, zinc, aluminum, lead, cadmium, and other metals; and
• sacrificial anodes or other corrosion-control materials.

Without this information, neither regulators nor the public can meaningfully assess the potential contamination footprint.

2. Evaluate corrosion and metal releases

Marine infrastructure requires corrosion protection. If sacrificial anodes containing aluminum, zinc, or other metals are used, those devices intentionally corrode over time.

FERC should require DeepGreen to quantify the total mass and chemical composition of all corrosion-control materials, expected annual release rates, lifetime metal releases, and potential accumulation in Cook Inlet water and sediments.

The analysis should include both major metals and trace constituents and should evaluate whether alternative corrosion-control methods could reduce environmental releases.

3. Analyze failure of subsea computing pods

The proposed data-center pods contain electronics, metals, polymers, coatings, wiring, and potentially batteries or specialized cooling materials.

DeepGreen should be required to model credible failure scenarios, including:

• loss of watertight integrity;
• cable or connector failure;
• internal fire or thermal event;
• battery failure, if batteries are present;
• vessel or anchor damage;
• seismic events;
• extreme tidal loading; and
• structural failure or loss of equipment.

For each scenario, DeepGreen should identify what materials could be released, in what quantities, how quickly contamination could spread, and how damaged equipment would be recovered.

The relevant question is not merely whether the equipment is designed not to leak. The environmental review must examine what happens when equipment fails.

4. Evaluate PFAS and other persistent chemicals

This comment does not assert that DeepGreen’s equipment contains PFAS or that PFAS would necessarily be released.

The concern is that the public record does not yet provide enough information to know.

DeepGreen should affirmatively disclose whether PFAS or other persistent fluorinated substances are present in cables, electronics, seals, cooling systems, fire-suppression systems, coatings, lubricants, dielectric materials, or other submerged components.

Where such substances are present, the applicant should identify quantities and evaluate potential release through degradation, abrasion, heat, or equipment failure.

5. Evaluate plastics and microplastic generation

Subsea cables, insulation, seals, coatings, electronics, and composite components may contain significant amounts of polymers.

Cook Inlet’s strong tidal currents, suspended sediments, ice, vibration, and long-term mechanical exposure could contribute to abrasion and degradation.

FERC should require evaluation of whether the project could generate microplastics or other polymer particles over its operating life.

6. Establish environmental baselines before installation

Meaningful environmental monitoring is impossible without reliable pre-project baseline data.

DeepGreen should conduct multi-season baseline testing before installation, including:

• dissolved and total metals;
• sediment metals;
• hydrocarbons;
• relevant PFAS compounds, where applicable;
• organic contaminants associated with coatings and electronics;
• microplastics;
• temperature; and
• other water-quality indicators identified through the materials inventory.

Sampling should include water, suspended sediment, bottom sediment, and appropriate biological indicators.

Testing locations should include the proposed project area, reference sites, and locations identified through hydrodynamic modeling as likely contaminant deposition zones.

7. Examine sediment and food-web impacts

Cook Inlet’s strong currents do not eliminate contamination risk.

Metals and other contaminants can bind to suspended particles, accumulate in bottom sediments, and later be resuspended. Some contaminants may also enter the marine food web.

FERC should require evaluation of contaminant fate, sediment accumulation, bioavailability, and potential bioaccumulation in benthic organisms, fish, marine mammals, and prey species.

8. Protect Cook Inlet beluga habitat

Cook Inlet supports the endangered Cook Inlet beluga whale, and NOAA has designated substantial portions of Cook Inlet as critical habitat.

Environmental review should therefore include not only turbine strike and underwater noise but also potential chemical and water-quality effects on belugas, their habitat, and their prey.

Chronic contaminant releases, sediment accumulation, and accidental equipment failures should all be included in this analysis.

9. Analyze thermal impacts

Subsea computing does not eliminate waste heat. It transfers that heat into the surrounding marine environment.

DeepGreen should quantify the thermal output of each computing pod and the cumulative heat load from all proposed pods.

FERC should require hydrodynamic modeling to determine whether localized or seasonal temperature changes could affect marine habitat.

10. Require a detailed retrieval, cleanup, and decommissioning plan

DeepGreen should demonstrate how damaged or abandoned subsea infrastructure would actually be recovered under Cook Inlet conditions.

The plan should identify:

• responsible parties;
• available vessels and lifting equipment;
• maximum response times;
• winter-response capability;
• contamination monitoring;
• damaged-equipment recovery procedures; and
• sediment remediation procedures if contamination occurs.

Because the applicant is a special-purpose entity, FERC should also require adequate financial assurance so that Alaska residents and taxpayers are not left responsible for cleanup if the company fails, restructures, or abandons the project.

Financial security should be sufficient to remove turbines, data-center pods, cables, and related equipment and to investigate and remediate contamination.

REQUEST

I respectfully request that FERC not allow this project to advance without requiring substantially more information regarding its potential chemical and environmental footprint.

At minimum, the applicant should be required to provide:

1. a complete chemical and materials inventory;
2. quantitative corrosion and metal-release analysis;
3. PFAS and emerging-contaminant disclosure;
4. computing-pod failure analysis;
5. water and sediment baseline testing;
6. contaminant fate and bioaccumulation analysis;
7. thermal-impact modeling;
8. long-term environmental monitoring;
9. emergency retrieval and contamination-response planning; and
10. binding financial assurance for removal and remediation.

The central question is straightforward:

What materials are being placed underwater, what will those materials become after years of exposure to Cook Inlet, what happens when equipment fails, how will contamination be detected and cleaned up, and who will pay for it?

Those questions should be answered before this project moves forward.

Respectfully submitted,
Cynthia Vanden Berg
Anchorage, AK
